{
  "image_name": "4d0ac758e615ea0f18426270a03713ac.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how glucose regulates glucagon secretion in the pancreatic islet through interactions among β-, δ-, and α-cells. Rising glucose stimulates the β-cell to release insulin and the δ-cell to release somatostatin. Both hormones then act on the neighboring α-cell: insulin binds the insulin receptor, and somatostatin binds the somatostatin receptor. These inputs converge to suppress α-cell signaling, shown as reduced cAMP and PKA activity, which in turn inhibits glucagon release.\n\nThe α-cell is drawn as the main target integrating multiple positive and negative influences. In addition to direct glucose sensing, it receives inhibitory neural input that appears to represent parasympathetic tone and GABAergic neurons. In contrast, adrenaline acting through a β-adrenergic receptor provides a stimulatory signal to the α-cell. Glucagon secretion is depicted as granule exocytosis from the α-cell, and the surrounding arrows emphasize that when glucose is high, paracrine inhibition from insulin and somatostatin predominates.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that glucagon output is not controlled by glucose alone but by a network in which β- and δ-cell hormones strongly restrain α-cell activity during glucose elevation.",
